I have gathered all the relevant information. Here is my complete report on how owner addresses are handled in this repository.

## Summary

This is a Spring PetClinic REST app. The owner request DTO is **generated at build time** from the OpenAPI spec (`OwnerFieldsDto` / `OwnerDto`), so there is no hand-written DTO `.java` file — the schema in `openapi.yml` is the source of truth. The `POST /api/owners` flow is implemented as a chain of small OfficeFloor function classes under `rest/function/owner/`. Address/postcode/locality/household logic lives in those functions plus the `mapper/Locality.java` helper.

Important note: the tests reference **structured address fields** (`addressLine1`, `addressLine2`) that do **not** yet exist in the OpenAPI schema or any main-source code — this looks like an in-progress/expected feature (the acceptance test `coreNormalizesAndComposesStructuredAddress` would currently fail).

---

## 1. Owner request DTO (address, city, postcode, firstName, lastName)

No hand-written DTO exists. It is generated from the OpenAPI `OwnerFields` schema. The mapper references the generated types `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.OwnerFieldsDto` and `OwnerDto`.

**Source of truth:** `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` (lines 1991-2070)

```yaml
OwnerFields:
  title: Owner fields
  description: Editable fields of a pet owner.
  type: object
  properties:
    firstName:
      type: string
      minLength: 1
      maxLength: 30
      pattern: "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}$"
      example: George
    lastName:
      type: string
      minLength: 1
      maxLength: 30
      pattern: "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}\\.?$"
    address:
      description: The postal address of the pet owner.
      type: string
      minLength: 1
      maxLength: 255
      pattern: '.*\S.*'
      example: '110 W. Liberty St.'
    city:
      description: The city of the pet owner.
      type: string
      minLength: 1
      maxLength: 80
      pattern: '.*\S.*'
    telephone:
      type: string
      minLength: 1
      maxLength: 20
    email:
      type: string
      format: email
      maxLength: 255
    postcode:
      description: Optional 4-digit postcode; when present it must be valid for the city's region.
      type: string
      example: '2000'
    registrationDate:
      type: string
      format: date
    birthDate:
      type: string
      format: date
    sharesHousehold:
      description: When true, allow creating this owner even if another owner shares the same last name and address.
      type: boolean
  required:
    - firstName
    - lastName
    - address
    - city
    - telephone
```

The persistent domain model (mirrors the DTO fields, has extra derived columns like `householdId`, `householdSize`, `customerCode`, `namesakeCount`):

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`** — key fields (full file read):
```java
@Column(name = "address")  @NotEmpty  private String address;
@Column(name = "city")     @NotEmpty  private String city;
@Column(name = "telephone")@NotEmpty  @Pattern(regexp="^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$"...) private String telephone;
@Column(name = "email")    private String email;   // setEmail lower-cases
@Column(name = "postcode") private String postcode;
@Column(name = "household_id")   private String householdId;
@Column(name = "household_size") private Integer householdSize;
@Column(name = "customer_code")  private String customerCode;
@Column(name = "namesake_count") private Integer namesakeCount;
```
`firstName`/`lastName` are inherited from `Person` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Person.java`).

---

## 2. Owner validation ("valid when it supplies address plus city")

The "must supply address plus city" rule is expressed as the OpenAPI `required: [firstName, lastName, address, city, telephone]` plus the non-blank pattern `'.*\S.*'` and `minLength: 1` on `address` and `city`. This generates Bean Validation annotations on `OwnerFieldsDto`, enforced by `@Valid @RequestBody` in the function classes below. There is no separate custom "address+city" validator class.

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/ValidateOwner.java`** (full contents) — runs validation before load so an invalid body is a 400 even when the owner doesn't exist:
```java
@Validated
public class ValidateOwner {
    public void service(@Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto request, Out<OwnerFieldsDto> validated) {
        validated.set(request);
    }
}
```

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/BuildOwner.java`** (full contents) — also `@Valid`, maps DTO→Owner, defaults/validates registrationDate:
```java
@Validated
public class BuildOwner {
    public void service(@Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto request, OwnerMapper ownerMapper,
            Out<Owner> built, Out<OwnerFieldsDto> requestOut) throws RegistrationDateInFutureException {
        Owner owner = ownerMapper.toOwner(request);
        if (owner.getRegistrationDate() == null) {
            owner.setRegistrationDate(LocalDate.now());
        } else if (owner.getRegistrationDate().isAfter(LocalDate.now())) {
            throw new RegistrationDateInFutureException("Registration date must not be later than today");
        }
        built.set(owner);
        requestOut.set(request);
    }
}
```

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/ApplyOwner.java`** (full contents) — the update path; copies request fields (incl. address, city) onto an existing Owner:
```java
public class ApplyOwner {
    public void service(@Val Owner owner, @Val OwnerFieldsDto request) {
        owner.setAddress(request.getAddress());
        owner.setCity(request.getCity());
        owner.setFirstName(request.getFirstName());
        owner.setLastName(request.getLastName());
        owner.setTelephone(request.getTelephone());
        owner.setEmail(request.getEmail());
    }
}
```

The model-level `@NotEmpty` on `address`, `city`, `telephone` in `Owner.java` is a second enforcement layer. (No address/city validation messages are defined in `src/main/resources/messages/*.properties`.)

---

## 3. Household hash, postcode validation, and locality logic

